\section{Separate Graph Simplification from Cell Selection}

Inside a combinational cone, the implementation problem is to compute a Boolean function at an acceptable cost. A Boolean network is a directed acyclic graph whose leaves are primary inputs or state bits and whose internal nodes compute local functions. When optimizing an ordinary synchronous design, a first boundary is often a register: the optimizer can treat its output as a leaf and its input as a sink. A change to the boundary itself is a sequential transformation, not ordinary Boolean rewriting.

An AND-inverter graph (AIG) uses two-input AND nodes and possibly complemented edges. It makes negation cheap in the representation, not necessarily in the physical circuit. Structural hashing can reuse a node whenever its canonical pair of input literals already exists. It does not, by itself, identify different graph shapes that compute the same function. Balancing changes an association to reduce depth; rewriting replaces a small subgraph with an equivalent one; resubstitution tries to express a result using functions already present. The \href{https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~alanmi/abc/}{Berkeley ABC project} documents a synthesis and verification system built around such representations. The \href{https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/latest/using_yosys/synthesis/abc.html}{Yosys ABC documentation} explains its own interface: it invokes ABC on extracted logic, not as a substitute for the whole HDL frontend and implementation flow.

