\section{Factor a Shared Cone and Prove the Boundary}

The following Verilog-2005 \cite{ieee1364verilog} module exposes the original and rewritten outputs at once. Its \texttt{equivalent} output is an explicit one-bit miter: it is one exactly when the two outputs agree.

\begin{verbatim}
module factor(input a, b, c, output original, rewritten, equivalent);
    assign original  = (a & b) | (a & c);
    assign rewritten = a & (b | c);
    assign equivalent = ~(original ^ rewritten);
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

For known bits, \(a=0\) forces both results to zero regardless of \(b,c\); \(a=1\) reduces both to \(b\lor c\). This covers all eight combinations. If this is the only output using the products, factoring replaces two ANDs and one OR with one OR and one AND, both with a worst-case depth of two two-input gates. The delay from \(a\) changes from two gates to one, but the delay from \(b,c\) is still two. If another output uses \(a\land b\), one eliminated node was not dead. Even the small gate count needs the context of the surrounding graph.

Pseudocode for evaluating such a rewrite:

\begin{verbatim}
leaves = choose_small_cut(root)          # freeze this cone's inputs
before = function_of(root, leaves)       # cache its local truth table
after = factor_common_literal(before)    # one candidate expression
if SAT(before XOR after): reject         # witness shows a wrong bit
delta = estimate_cost_change(after)      # count only newly dead nodes
if delta meets policy: splice(after)     # preserve every observed sink
\end{verbatim}

The \emph{Handbook of Satisfiability}, in its circuit and hardware-verification discussions, motivates turning the difference between two networks into a SAT query. The XOR is the one-output comparison, or \emph{miter}. With Yosys, a compact check on the module above is:

\begin{verbatim}
read_verilog factor.v
prep -top factor
sat -verify -prove equivalent 1 -show-inputs
\end{verbatim}

This proves that the miter is one for all represented Boolean inputs. It assumes neither a latency change nor four-state simulation equivalence. A care condition such as a proved one-hot invariant can make more rewrites legal; simply assuming that a troublesome input cannot occur would be a specification change.

\Cref{fig:visual-314} shows why counting nodes alone does not describe the transformation: the path from \(a\) changes differently from the paths from \(b\) and \(c\).

