\section{Optimize for a Metric, Then Check That Metric}

An ASIC library might provide a combined gate and multiple drive strengths; a four-input FPGA LUT can implement either entire cone. Ranking expressions by AIG node count or logic levels therefore gives a proxy, not a mapped area or delay. Gate sizing, load from fanout, and routing can even make it useful to duplicate equivalent logic near distant consumers. Switching activity and glitches introduce still different cost measures. Two equivalent steady-state truth tables need not have equal transient activity.

Useful optimization scripts alternate several cheap passes rather than solving global minimization. Cut enumeration is bounded; a small cut cannot expose a simplification across a larger one. Exact SAT checks can also grow expensive and a timeout is not a proof either way. Finally, when debugging a regression, keep the same top, target, and constraints and compare the same artifact stage. Fewer generic gates before mapping is useful diagnostic information; it is not a claim about the number of placed LUTs or the timing of a routed design.
